{"rules":[{"jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","level":"city","category":"rent_increase_limits","status":"in_force","title":"Los Angeles Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO) - allowable annual rent increases","requirement":"Rent for RSO units may be increased once every 12 months by the allowable rent increase percentage published by LAHD. Since February 2, 2026 no additional percentage for utilities is allowed, and the 10% increase for an additional dependent is no longer permitted. A 10% increase for an additional non-dependent tenant is allowed if the landlord acts within 60 days of learning of the tenant. Rent may be reset to market rate on vacancy-type events (voluntary move-out, eviction for nonpayment or lease violation, failure to comply with a Tenant Habitability Plan, City Attorney order, or a Tenant Buyout Agreement). Further increases above the allowable percentage require LAHD approval (Capital Improvement, Primary Renovation, Seismic Retrofit, Rehabilitation, Just and Reasonable Rent Increase). Rent increases were prohibited from 2020-03-30 through 2024-01-31. Landlords may also pass through registration, Systematic Code Enforcement fee and smoke detector surcharges.","key_value":"One increase per 12 months, capped at the LAHD-published allowable percentage (the percentage is not stated in the document)","coverage_conditions":"Rental properties first built on or before October 1, 1978, plus replacement units under LAMC Section 151.28. Covers apartments, condominiums, townhomes, duplexes, two or more single-family units on one parcel, hotel/motel/rooming-house rooms occupied by the same tenant for more than 30 consecutive days, residential units attached to a commercial building, ADUs, JADUs, and mobile homes and RVs in mobile home parks.","exemptions":"Rent amount is not regulated for condominium or townhome tenancies that commenced after December 31, 1995. Landlords can apply for a Luxury Exemption. Temporary exemptions also exist.","coverage_logic":{"covers":{"field":"certificate_of_occupancy","op":"<=","value":"1978-10-01"},"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":null,"citation":"Los Angeles Rent Stabilization Ordinance (LAMC Section 151.28)","quoted_span":"Rent may be increased once every 12 months by the allowable rent increase percentage (click here to","supporting_spans":["Generally, the RSO applies to rental properties that were first built on or before October 1, 1978, as well as replacement units under","Effective February 2, 2026, the landlord can no longer include an additional percentage increase for utilities."],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_note":"The page gives two figures for the registration surcharge ($1.61 and $38.75) and the allowable percentage is not stated, so the text appears partly outdated or garbled."},{"jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","level":"city","category":"just_cause_eviction","status":"in_force","title":"Los Angeles RSO - legal reasons for eviction and relocation assistance","requirement":"RSO tenancies may be ended only for listed reasons. At-fault reasons are failure to fix a violation of the rental agreement and creating a nuisance or damaging the unit. No-fault reasons are owner or immediate family move-in, resident manager move-in, demolition and permanent removal from the rental market, and government order. No-fault evictions require a Landlord Declaration of Intent to Evict filed with LAHD, written notice of 30 and 60 days (some evictions require 120 days or up to a 1-year extension), and payment of tenant relocation assistance. All termination notices must be filed with LAHD within three business days of service on the tenant. Landlords must also attach the RSO notice to any eviction notice.","key_value":"Notice of 30 and 60 days for no-fault evictions (some require 120 days or up to a 1-year extension), with relocation assistance; termination notices filed with LAHD within 3 business days","coverage_conditions":"Rental units subject to the Rent Stabilization Ordinance (generally built on or before October 1, 1978, per the RSO coverage list).","exemptions":null,"coverage_logic":{"covers":{"field":"certificate_of_occupancy","op":"<=","value":"1978-10-01"},"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":null,"citation":"Los Angeles Rent Stabilization Ordinance (LAMC 151.09.C.9 & 165.05.B.5)","quoted_span":"Give the tenant a 30-day and 60-day written notice (some evictions require 120-day notice or up to a 1-year extension).","supporting_spans":["Failure to fix or address a violation of the rental agreement","Demolition and permanent removal from the rental market"],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_note":null}],"notes":"The page lists 'Interest Payments on Security Deposits' as an RSO topic, but gives no operative detail (rate, deadline, or cap) and no quotable sentence, so no security_deposits rule was recorded. No other in-scope categories appear. Effective and enactment dates for the RSO itself are not given. The Feb 2, 2026 changes are amendments to existing rules and are folded into the rent increase record."}
